course: everyday
lesson: 497
title: Tired, sad or drinking parents: tell a trusted adult
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes a grown-up at home is not okay, and a child sees it first. In Cardiff, Carys's mum works long shifts and falls asleep on the sofa at seven every night, still in her uniform. Carys makes her own tea (in much of Britain «tea» also means the evening meal), does her own bath and puts herself to bed. «S'fine, Mam's just cream-crackered,» she tells her friend («cream-crackered» is old rhyming slang for «knackered», very tired). Carys feels grown-up, and lonely. Mum loves her; she just has nothing left at the end of the day. But a child still needs looking after.
explain: Some grown-ups have an illness called depression. It is not just a bad day: it makes a person very sad, flat and tired for weeks or months. In Hull, Angus's dad stays in bed for days with the curtains shut: «Sorry, mate, just tired.» In Brisbane, Rafferty's mum stays in her room, so every arvo (afternoon) and evening he makes toast for himself and his little sister. In Bristol, since he lost his job, Jem's dad watches telly all day, doesn't wash and snaps at everyone; Jem's mate says, «Your dad's gert mardy» (Bristol «gert» = very, «mardy» = grumpy). Depression is an illness, not laziness, and doctors can help. It is not the child's job to fix it.
explain: Some grown-ups drink too much alcohol, and it changes how they act. In Manchester, Lettie's mum is slurring her words and stumbling at tea time, and she forgets Lettie's school play. In Ohio, Everett's dad drinks every night, gets loud, yells at the TV and at Everett's mom, so Everett stays in his room. In Dublin, Hester can't wake her da in the afternoon to sign a school form, so she writes his name herself. None of it is the child's fault, and no child should cope with it alone.
explain: Not every hard thing is drink or depression. In Edinburgh, Olivia hears her mam greetin' (Scots for crying) at night since Granny died, and in the morning Mam pretends to be fine. Grief can last a long time. In Leeds, Maddox's mum has a long illness and can't come to sports day; Maddox tells his mates, «She's busy,» because he wants to protect her. Kids who look after a poorly or struggling parent are called young carers, and there are groups and help for them.
explain: And some grown-ups are simply mean to a child. In Newcastle, Mam's new boyfriend calls Romilly names and blames her for everything, and Mam says nothing. «Howay, he's horrible,» Romilly whispers to her teddy («howay» is Geordie for «come on»). That is not okay, and she is not to blame. The wise move in every story here is the same: tell a trusted adult — a teacher, a school counsellor, a nan, an auntie — or ring Childline on 0800 1111 (UK, free). It is not telling tales and it is not betraying your family: it is getting help for them and for you.
fact: When a parent is very tired, ill, sad or drinking, a child often notices first.
fact: Depression is an illness that makes a person very sad, flat and tired for a long time; it is not laziness and doctors can help.
fact: It is not a child's job to fix a parent's depression or drinking.
fact: Drinking too much alcohol changes how people act: slurring words, stumbling, forgetting, getting loud.
fact: A parent's drinking, depression or bad mood is never the child's fault.
fact: Grief after someone dies can make a grown-up sad for a long time; if it goes on and on, they may need help.
fact: Children who cook, mind siblings or look after a struggling parent are called young carers, and there are groups to help them.
fact: A grown-up who keeps calling a child names and blaming her is not okay, and the child is not to blame.
fact: Telling a trusted adult, like a teacher, school counsellor or relative, is getting help, not telling tales.
fact: Childline is free on 0800 1111 in the UK; in the US kids can tell a school counselor or call Childhelp on 1-800-422-4453.
fact: Hiding a problem, like forging a signature or saying «she's busy», keeps the child alone with it; telling helps.
fact: «Cream-crackered» means very tired; «greetin'» is Scots for crying; «gert» is Bristol for very; «mardy» means grumpy; «howay» is Geordie for come on; «arvo» is Australian for afternoon.
exercise: Carys's mum falls asleep at seven every night after her shift. What does Carys do? | Makes her own tea and puts herself to bed | Goes out to the park on her own till midnight | Rings the fire brigade to wake her mum up | Eats sweets for tea and stays up all night
exercise: Why is Carys's mum so tired? | She works long shifts | She doesn't love Carys any more | She was out partying | Carys keeps her awake
exercise: What is depression? | An illness that makes a person very sad and tired for a long time | Just being a bit lazy and not wanting to get up on a Monday | A bad mood that always goes away by the next morning | Something a child causes by being noisy in the house
exercise: Angus's dad stays in bed for days with the curtains shut. Whose job is it to fix it? | Doctors' and grown-ups', not Angus's | Angus's, by telling better jokes every day | Nobody's; it fixes itself in one day | His teacher's, by giving Angus extra homework
exercise: Rafferty makes toast for himself and his sister every night. What is he? | A young carer | A chef | A babysitter for pay | In trouble
exercise: What did Lettie see at tea time? | Mum slurring and stumbling | Mum cooking a big roast dinner for her | Mum dancing at the school play | Mum reading her a story
exercise: What should Lettie do? | Tell a trusted adult or ring Childline | Pour Mum's drinks down the sink every day | Keep it a secret for ever to protect Mum | Stay home from school to look after Mum
exercise: Everett's dad drinks and yells. Everett stays in his room. Who can Everett tell in Ohio? | A teacher or school counselor | Nobody at all | Just his dog | Only the boy next door
exercise: Hester wrote Da's name on the form herself. What would help more? | Telling her teacher | Getting better at copying it | Never bringing forms home again | Throwing all forms in the bin
exercise: Olivia's mam cries at night since Granny died. What is it? | Grief, which can last a long time | Depression caused by Olivia's noise | A cold that will pass by Tuesday | Mam being dramatic for attention
exercise: Why does Maddox say his mum is «busy»? | To protect her and hide the illness | Because she really is at work | Because he's ashamed of his own sports | Because his mates asked him to lie
exercise: Mam's boyfriend calls Romilly names and blames her for everything. Is it her fault? | No, a grown-up being mean to a child is not okay | Yes, she must be doing something wrong all day | Yes, if Mam doesn't stop him it must be fair | Only a bit, because she's sometimes cheeky
exercise: What is the free Childline number in the UK? | 0800 1111 | 999 | 0800 1234 | 111
exercise: Is telling a teacher about home «telling tales»? | No, it's getting help for you and your family | Yes, and it will get the whole family arrested | Yes, family things must never leave the house | Only if you tell more than one teacher about it
exercise: «Mam's cream-crackered.» What does that mean? | She's very tired | She's eating crackers | She's cross | She's gone out
exercise: Jem's dad lost his job and now doesn't wash and snaps at everyone. What might it be? | Depression, and he needs help, not blame | Just a holiday that he's really enjoying | Jem's fault for asking for new trainers | A new job he does from the sofa at home
test: «my dad's just lazy, he's been in bed since Sunday, can't be arsed». What might really be going on? | He may have depression, an illness | He is definitely just lazy, nothing more | He's waiting for Jem to make him get up | He is training to sleep for a world record
test: «if I make Mum laugh enough she'll stop being sad, sorted». True? | No, it isn't your job to fix it; tell a grown-up | Yes, enough jokes always cure depression | Yes, but only if the jokes are really good | No, she should never laugh while she's sad
test: Hester's mate says, «just sign it yourself every time, they'll never know lol». Why is that a bad plan? | It keeps Hester alone with the problem | Teachers can read minds about forms | It's fine, nothing bad ever comes of it | Forms don't really need signing anyway
test: «my mum's boyfriend says I'm useless n it's all my fault». What should she do? | Tell a teacher or ring Childline | Believe him and try to be less useless | Shout back the same things at him | Run away from home that night alone
test: In a TV show a boy cooks every night for his sad mum and tells no one, and it's called «heroic». What does the lesson add? | Young carers are brave but need help too | Keeping it secret is the best thing to do | Only grown-ups ever cook in real homes | Cooking every night is easy for any kid
test: What would probably happen if Maddox told his teacher about his mum's illness? | He could get support, like a young carers group | He would get his mum into trouble with the police | He would have to leave his school straight away | Nothing, teachers aren't allowed to help at all
test: «Granny died ages ago, Mam should be over it by now innit». What is true? | Grief can last a long time | Grief always ends in a week | Grown-ups don't feel grief | Crying means Mam is ill
test: Everett's buddy says, «Your pop yells 'cause you're annoying, bro». What is true? | Drinking changes how people act; it isn't Everett's fault | Everett must be the reason his dad drinks every night | Everett should be quieter, then the yelling will stop | All dads yell at night, so it's nothing to talk about
test: ★★★ Carys is alone each evening from 7 pm, when Mum falls asleep, until 9 pm, when Carys goes to bed. Mum works 5 nights a week, and only on those nights does she fall asleep at 7. How many hours a week is Carys on her own in the evening? | 10 | 7 | 14 | 2
test: ★★★ Which of these is NOT a good person to tell about a worry at home? | A stranger met in an online game | A teacher | A school counsellor | Childline
test: «telling about my dad = betraying the family». What does the lesson say? | Telling is getting help for your family and you | It is betraying, so it's better to say nothing | Only tell if Dad says it's okay to tell anyone | Tell only your friends, never any grown-ups
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises depression? | An illness occasioning prolonged sadness and fatigue | A disposition to idleness which may be remedied by effort | A transient indisposition which resolves within a day | A condition caused principally by the conduct of children
exam: Identify the course of action recommended where a child observes a parent slurring, stumbling and neglecting engagements owing to alcohol. | Disclosure to a trusted adult or to Childline | Concealment of the matter in order to protect the parent | Disposal of the parent's alcohol by the child himself | Absence from school so as to supervise the parent
exam: State the term by which a child who habitually cooks for siblings and attends to an unwell parent is designated. | A young carer | A guardian ad litem appointed by the court | A domestic servant | An apprentice of the household
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an adult who repeatedly disparages and blames a child within the home. | Such conduct is unacceptable and the child bears no blame | Such conduct is acceptable where the parent raises no objection | The child is presumed to have provoked such conduct | Such conduct must remain undisclosed beyond the household
